+### Optimizing for a Viewing Area
+
+`--filter-view-score` renders the scene from a set of cameras and scores every Gaussian by the pixels it effectively paints (the sum of its blending weights over the image). Gaussians that are hidden, out of view or too faint to register in every view score 0.
+
+```bash
+# Drop everything a camera path never sees (the path's views are unchanged).
+# The track is a supersplat project's document.json, a viewer settings.json or a frame list.
+splat-transform --camera-track document.json input.ply --filter-view-score output.sog
+
+# Also drop Gaussians that paint at most a quarter pixel in their best view,
+# scoring at the resolution the scene will be shown at
+splat-transform --camera-track views.json --resolution 1280x720 input.ply --filter-view-score 0.25 output.sog
+
+# A single static viewpoint
+splat-transform --camera-pos 0,1.6,4 --camera-target 0,1.6,0 input.ply --filter-view-score output.spz
+```
+
+Scores are in pixels of the scoring image, so a threshold above 0 depends on `--resolution` and on how close the cameras are: score at the size and distance the scene will be viewed at. The result is tailored to the scored views. From anywhere else the scene shows holes, so restrict the viewer's camera accordingly. A threshold above 0 removes detail rather than merging it; raise it in small steps and compare renders.
+
+A frame list is a JSON file: `{ "frames": [{ "position": [x, y, z], "target": [x, y, z], "fov": 60 }, ...] }`.
+
